.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_ADD 3 "2026-09-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_add \- register a selectable with an event loop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_event_reg ccol_event_loop_add(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_selectable sel,
                       ccol_event_handlers_t handlers, void *arg,
                       char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function registers sel with loop, and the loop then dispatches
the readiness of sel to handlers for the whole life of the
registration, or until a call to ccol_event_loop_remove().

You build sel exactly as you build one for ccol_select(), and you can
use ccol_selectable_from_fd, ccol_selectable_from_circq,
ccol_selectable_from_dynq and ccol_selectable_from_chan directly.

One registration covers exactly one direction, which is sel.dir. If you
want both directions live on one fd at the same time (for example a
full-duplex pipe that a program reads and writes together), call
ccol_event_loop_add() twice and you get two independent handles. If you
want to change the direction of one registration over time (for example
a connecting socket, which wants write interest until the connect
finishes and read interest after that), use one registration and
ccol_event_loop_modify(3). The function refuses a second selectable for
a direction that is already in use on the same fd, such as two read
registrations on one fd; the call then returns CCOL_EVENT_REG_NULL
and err_str names that cause (see RETURN VALUE).

A queue selectable and a ccol_channel selectable have no such
restriction. You can make any number of ccol_event_loop_add() calls for
the same queue and the same direction, in one ccol_event_loop or in
several, and mix them with a ccol_select() call that runs at the same
time on the same queue and direction. All of them are live together,
and each one gets its turn. ccol_select() gives the same guarantee: any
number of threads can watch one queue at the same time.

When a message arrives while more than one such listener is registered,
the library wakes exactly one listener for each producer call and each
consumer call. Once the callback of the woken listener returns, that
listener checks whether the queue is ready for its direction, and if it
is, it passes the wake to the next listener in the line, so no live
listener is passed over for ever. A ccol_select() caller that wakes and
then leaves without acting on the queue passes the wake on in the same
way, and so does a registration that is removed, or whose loop is shut
down or destroyed, before it acted on its wake, so no wake is lost. A
registration of a loop that is shut down takes no further wakes, and the
other listeners of the queue keep getting every message while it waits
for its remove or for the destroy of its loop.

A registration whose callback moved at least one message is dispatched
again for as long as the queue stays ready for its direction. Moved
means that for a read registration the queue holds fewer messages after
the callback than before it, and for a write registration it holds
more. This holds for each registration on its own, wherever it sits in
the line and whatever the other listeners of the queue do. So a backlog
drains without another send or receive, even when each callback
receives one message, and a listener that declines the queue never
holds up one that takes it. A registration whose callback neither
receives nor sends passes the wake on, and a wake that every listener
declines stops once it has visited each of them, so listeners that
ignore a ready queue do not spin. A write registration whose callback
sends on every call is dispatched again for as long as the queue has
room, exactly as a writable fd is, so remove it when it has nothing to
send. A queue whose sends are turned off is ready for a write
registration, in the way a pipe whose reader has gone is ready for
POLLOUT. Turning sends off calls on_writable once, a write registration
that is added while sends are off gets one call as soon as it is added,
and the send of the callback then returns ccol_not_permitted, which
moves nothing, so the wake is passed on as described above. The
registration is called again only after a later event on the queue,
such as a receive or another change of the send state.

A queue that you register this way must live longer than its
registration. ccol_circular_queue_destroy(3),
ccol_dynamic_queue_destroy(3) and ccol_channel_destroy(3) assert if a
live registration, a ccol_select() call or a ccol_select_timed() call
watches the queue; without those asserts the waiter would stay linked
and point to the mutex of the queue, which the destroy is about to
free. Call ccol_event_loop_remove() before you destroy the queue.
Destroying the loop first is fine, because ccol_event_loop_destroy()
and ccol_event_loop_remove() always unlink the waiter of a queue before
anything touches it again. ccol_event_loop_remove() does not wait for a
callback that it already gave to a reactor thread, so the destroy that
comes after it blocks until any such callback is done with the queue.
The page of that destroy names the two things that a caller must not do
while it waits.

on_readable, on_writable and on_error share one prototype:

    void fn(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg,
            ccol_selectable *sel, void *arg);

loop is the loop that owns the registration, sel is its selectable and
arg is the arg of this call. reg is the handle of the registration
itself, exactly the value that ccol_event_loop_add() returns for it,
and a callback can pass it to ccol_event_loop_remove(3),
ccol_event_loop_pause(3), ccol_event_loop_resume(3),
ccol_event_loop_modify(3) and ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(3). The
registration is live, and a callback of it can run, before
ccol_event_loop_add() returns: with num_reactor_threads > 1 a callback
can run on a dispatch worker while the calling thread is inside this
function, for example when the fd is ready already at the time of the
call. reg is valid in that callback too, so a callback never has to
read the handle from storage that the calling thread writes after this
call returns.

handlers.on_readable reports readiness only, for every type of
selectable, and ccol_event_loop never does the receive itself. For an
fd selectable the callback makes its own read(2) or recv(2) call on
sel->fd. For a queue selectable or a ccol_channel selectable, the
callback makes its own ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() or
ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc() call on sel->cq or sel->dq. For a ccol_channel
selectable that call is ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() on sel->cq, and never
ccol_chan_try_recv_zc(): the callback runs on a thread of the loop,
which the channel counts as a worker, so a ccol_chan_* call there can
reach the other queue of the channel (see ccol_selectable_from_chan(3)).
In both cases another consumer can get to the data first. That TOCTOU
race is part of the design and is the same contract as the write
direction of ccol_select(): the receive call of the callback can find
nothing, and the callback must handle that well.

For a queue selectable one notification does not always mean exactly
one message, because several sends can run faster than the dispatch,
and the callback then sees them as one notification. A callback that
receives one message on each call drains a burst all the same, because
the loop dispatches the listeners again while the queue stays ready
after a callback that moved a message (see above). A callback can also
receive in a loop until the call returns ccol_container_empty, which
drains a burst in fewer dispatches.

handlers.on_writable runs with no payload, for both kinds of
selectable. The caller makes its own write(2) or send(2) call, or for a
queue its own try-send call, which does not block:
ccol_circq_try_send_zc() on sel->cq for a circular queue or a
ccol_channel selectable, and ccol_dynmq_send_zc() on sel->dq for a
dynamic queue.

handlers.on_error runs only for an fd selectable, for each condition
that the direction of the registration has no other handler for.
EPOLLERR and EPOLLHUP go there whenever the direction is not also
ready. For a read registration with on_readable set to NULL, the close
or the shutdown of the peer's end goes there too; that is EPOLLRDHUP,
which an ordinary TCP close(2) raises without EPOLLHUP.

If the direction does have its own handler, that handler wins the same
event whenever the direction is also ready. When data arrives together
with the error, a read registration with on_readable set gets
on_readable and not on_error: EPOLLIN and EPOLLRDHUP are not mutually
exclusive with EPOLLERR and EPOLLHUP, and the loop delivers the bytes
that arrived before the peer went away. For the same reason a write
registration with on_writable set gets on_writable, since EPOLLOUT is
not mutually exclusive with EPOLLERR and EPOLLHUP either: a socket in
an error state is writable too, because a write(2) call on it returns
an error at once and does not block. A queue selectable never produces
an error condition.

A registration with on_error set to NULL gets EPOLLERR and EPOLLHUP
through the handler of its own direction instead, as libevent and libuv
deliver them: on_readable for a read registration, on_writable for a
write registration. The read(2), recv(2), write(2) or send(2) call of
that handler then reports the condition, with -1 and the error in
errno, or with 0 for an end of file. A read or a write of a socket also
clears a pending socket error, so a transient error (such as the ICMP
port unreachable that a connected UDP socket reports as ECONNREFUSED)
costs one failed receive, and the datagrams that arrive after it reach
on_readable as usual. When both directions of one fd have such a
handler, one event reaches both: on_readable first, then on_writable,
one after the other on one thread, and each one sees whatever the other
left of the condition.

Each of on_readable, on_writable and on_error can be NULL. For an fd
selectable the loop does not arm a direction that has no handler of its
own, so a registration can carry on_error alone and leave the handler
of its own direction NULL. Such an error-only registration costs no
dispatch until something goes wrong, in both directions and for every
kind of fd that the loop watches (a socket, a pipe, a FIFO or a
terminal). That includes an error-only registration on the write
direction of a healthy socket, which is writable from the moment it is
registered, and one on either end of a pipe, where the close of the
other end is the error that it reports.

Every condition here, an error included, is level-triggered: the loop
reports it, and calls the handler, again and again until a call removes
or pauses the registration or the condition clears. A hang-up stays
true, so a pipe whose writer closed, or a socket whose peer is gone,
keeps reporting it, and a read(2) of it keeps returning 0. A handler
that reads an end of file must therefore remove the registration (or
pause it), exactly as it must for any other readiness that it leaves in
place. Remove it before you close the fd; see ccol_event_loop_remove(3).
On the BSDs and macOS an error-only registration (on_error alone) can
hear each error or hang-up once, when it happens, and not on every
wait, because the kernel filter that watches it there can be
edge-triggered; a handler that removes or pauses the registration works
the same on every system.

An fd event that no handler of the registration can take is dropped,
and the loop also stops watching that registration, because the hang-up
behind it can stay true and would otherwise be reported on every wait.
Only a registration with neither the handler of its direction nor
on_error ends up there, for example a read registration with both
on_readable and on_error set to NULL whose peer hangs up. The
registration stays registered, and a registration for the other
direction of the same fd stays watched. ccol_event_loop_resume(3) and a
successful ccol_event_loop_modify(3) arm it again, whether the modify
changes the direction or keeps it.

handlers.on_removed, unlike the other three, is not tied to a readiness
event. It runs exactly once, at the moment when the library can prove
that the caller can free arg. After ccol_event_loop_remove() it runs
asynchronously and never from inside that call, while
ccol_event_loop_destroy(3) runs every on_removed that is due from
inside the call, on the destroying thread, before it returns. The NOTES
of ccol_event_loop_remove(3) give the exact guarantee and explain why
ccol_event_loop_remove() itself cannot always give it synchronously.
You can leave on_removed NULL for a registration whose arg needs no
notice before a free. Calling ccol_event_loop_destroy(3) on the owning
loop from on_removed is fatal: on an ordinary removal on_removed runs
on the reactor thread, where a destroy of its own loop is refused, and
during a destroy the handle is already destroyed.

A callback runs on one of the background reactor threads of loop and
never on the thread that calls this function, and how long a callback
can take depends on how the loop is configured. With the default of one
reactor thread, that one thread both polls and runs the callbacks, so a
callback that blocks stalls every other registration on the loop for
as long as it blocks; keep such a callback short and give slow work to
a thread of your own. With more than one reactor thread there is one
dedicated poller and the callbacks run on a separate dispatch pool,
which is what that configuration is for, so a callback can take real
time without starving the polling. A callback holds no internal lock
of the loop while it runs, so it can take an application lock of its
own, and it can call ccol_event_loop_modify(3), ccol_event_loop_pause(3)
and ccol_event_loop_remove(3). Those calls keep working while
ccol_event_loop_shutdown(3) or ccol_event_loop_destroy(3) drains the
loop, because loop stays a live handle until that drain ends. So a
callback that removes every registration of its fd and then frees arg
is safe during a destroy on another thread: no callback of those
registrations runs after the removals return.

With more than one reactor thread, the loop never runs the callback of
one registration concurrently with itself (see num_reactor_threads in
ccol_event_loop_create_mp(3)). A read registration and a write
registration that share one fd never run at the same time as each other
either, so a pair of callbacks that shares state across both directions
of one fd (such as one TLS connection object) needs no lock of its own
for that. This rule does not extend to two different fds: the callbacks
of different registrations can run at the same time on different
reactor threads.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop      ccol_event_loop to register with.
sel       What to watch. See DESCRIPTION.
handlers  Group of callbacks. Each callback can be NULL.
arg       Opaque pointer that the loop gives to every callback of this
          registration.
err_str   Optional pointer that gets an error string on failure, and
          NULL on success.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A new registration handle on success.

CCOL_EVENT_REG_NULL on failure. The call fails when loop is
CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_NULL, a stale handle or a handle that has already
been destroyed; when sel is malformed because its type or dir is not
valid, its queue pointer is NULL or its fd is negative; when loop is
shut down (ccol_event_loop_shutdown(3)) and nothing of it dispatches
any more; when the direction is already in use on that fd; or when an
allocation or a system call failed (epoll_ctl or kevent, eventfd, or
the initialisation of a mutex or a condition variable). An fd that
names a regular file or a directory is always refused this way,
because epoll(7) cannot watch such a file (epoll_ctl(2) fails with
EPERM). The FreeBSD build refuses it too, even though kqueue(2) could
watch it, so that the same program behaves the same on both systems.

When err_str is not NULL, *err_str names the cause, with one message
per class. A loop that is shut down gives a message containing "shut
down". A direction already in use gives one containing "direction
already in use", and no retry can succeed in that case. A failed
allocation gives one containing "failed to allocate", and a failed
system call one containing "a system call failed".
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Like ccol_event_loop itself, ccol_event_reg is an opaque VALUE handle
and not a pointer. Never cast it to or from void*, never compare it
through a pointer cast, and never treat it as an address. Compare it
with CCOL_EVENT_REG_NULL, or test it for truth, since
CCOL_EVENT_REG_NULL is 0. Every use of a ccol_event_reg is resolved
against its loop's registration table before anything is dereferenced,
so a stale or removed handle is always caught and refused cleanly,
never turned into a use-after-free. That holds even while another
thread races a ccol_event_loop_modify, ccol_event_loop_pause,
ccol_event_loop_resume, ccol_event_loop_remove or
ccol_event_loop_reg_generation call on the very same registration.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call at the same time as ccol_event_loop_remove() and
ccol_event_loop_modify(), and from a callback running on the reactor
thread.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void on_msg(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg, ccol_selectable *sel,
            void *arg) {
    (void)loop; (void)reg; (void)arg;
    c_message_t msg;
    if (ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(sel->cq, &msg) != ccol_success) return;
    printf("job: %.*s\en", (int)msg.size, (char *)msg.data);
    free(msg.data);
}

ccol_circular_queue *jobs = ccol_circular_queue_create(64, NULL);
ccol_event_handlers_t handlers = { .on_readable = on_msg };
ccol_event_reg reg = ccol_event_loop_add(
    loop, ccol_selectable_from_circq(jobs, ccol_select_read),
                               handlers, NULL, NULL);
.fi
